@demographics
go-to: menu

**Demographic Groups**

**Industrial Workers**: The urban proletariat - factory workers, miners, and dockworkers, concentrated in Madrid, Barcelona, the Basque Country, and Asturias. Most support either the <span style="color: #c00000;">**PSOE**</span> and the <span style="color: #700000;">**UGT**</span>, or the anarcho-syndicalist <span style="color: #333333;">**CNT**</span>, with a smaller but growing <span style="color: #700000;">**PCE**</span> following. Roughly a quarter of the population.

**Smallholders**: Small farmers, independent artisans, and shopkeepers, concentrated in the conservative Catholic north and centre (Old Castile, Navarre). They tend to be conservative and are the strongest rural base for the <span style="color: #003755;">**CEDA**</span> and the monarchists. 10-15% of the population.

**Urban Middle Class**: White-collar workers, professionals, and civil servants. This is the base of republican liberalism - the <span style="color: #4a7d3a;">**Republican Left**</span> and the <span style="color: #d9a441;">Radical Party</span>. 10-15% of the population.

**Landless Laborers**: The *braceros* - landless day laborers on the great estates (*latifundios*) of Andalusia and Extremadura. Among the poorest and most radicalized groups in Spain, they are the <span style="color: #c00000;">**PSOE**</span>'s strongest rural base, though many are drawn instead to <span style="color: #333333;">**CNT**</span> anarchism and its promise of land seizure. 20-25% of the population.

**Practicing Catholics** cut across class lines and are the single largest bloc aligned with the <span style="color: #003755;">**CEDA**</span>, whose Catholic-authoritarian politics and defense of the Church give it a following well beyond the smallholder and landowning classes. Roughly 30% of the population identify as devout, churchgoing Catholics, concentrated in the north and centre.


